Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.910 align:start position:0%
 
it's been a bit of a strange show today  because we've had tony blair  uh saying that the eu have behaved  foolishly rachel reeves there saying  they haven't covered themselves in glory  both remain backers  and yet liz trust who voted for brexit  was kind of stepping back from  criticizing them what's your  view on the eu's actions over the  vaccine supply this week  well they got it completely wrong and i  think the government is being very wise  to  lower the temperature in a situation  like this because  this year the big threat which is not  something many people predicted  is around these new variants  particularly from south africa and also  brazil and if we are going to tackle  these new variants and they could  happen to be south africa and brazil  this time it could be anywhere else next  time we are going to need to have high  levels of  close cooperation and collaboration  across the world  we all know the problems that happen  because we didn't find  out about the virus in china as quickly  as we might have  so we really do need to be working  closely with everyone and we have the  capacity to do that in this country with  our  world-beating genomics capacity so  that's why i think this was a huge wrong  step for the eu to take but i think it's  very welcome that it's been resolved  you're talking there about the threat  from other variants springing up around  the world  of course we only know the ones that  have been discovered there may be more  do you think that means that once the uk  is vaccinated the most vulnerable  we should look at vaccinating people in  other countries as well  yes i mean we've got 367 million doses  we won't need  all of those right now we're in a race  because we do have the the uk variant we  have some of the south african variants  some of the brazilian variants here  and we need to vaccinate uh if we  possibly can  the whole adult population but certainly  to the levels to get herd immunity  before some of these new variants really  take hold because  some of the ones in the future may in  fact be immune to the vaccine and what  you want to do is to get transmission  as low as possible so when those new  variants  arrive you can really do the detailed  contact tracing what they call the deep  contact tracing to isolate  people very very quickly and deep but  you can only do that when transmission  levels are very low so it's a race  against time  at the moment but i have to say the  vaccine program is going extremely well  and i think we've every chance of  succeeding in that  um once transmission is down to  what we hope to be very low levels what  other countries should we prioritize  giving vaccines to are you thinking of  like the lowest income countries the  ones with most travel to the uk  the ones who are our closest neighbours  what's your view  well i think it goes without saying that  we're not safe until everyone's safe and  i think  one of the one of the parts of the world  where we could make the biggest  difference is africa  and in particular the oxford astrazeneca  vaccine that we developed in this  country  has the biggest potential to be  transformational in africa because it  can be transported at low temperatures  and it's very cheap and indeed  astrazeneca have said they don't want to  make any money from it  so costs will be much less of an issue  so i think with our large aid budget and  our huge connections in africa  that is certainly a part of the world  where the uk could make an enormous  difference  we all know of course that one of the  biggest threats at the minute is the  emergence of these new variants  some of them are more transmissible some  of them there are concerns about  vaccine efficacy now four weeks ago on  the fourth of january you tweeted  calling for international travel to be  stopped she said  we cannot afford to wait now in those  four weeks  nine cases of the brazilian variant have  been found in the united kingdom  the number of cases of the south african  variant we believe has  more than trebled i mean i know these  figures you know their estimates  but things have clearly gone in the  wrong direction  were we much much too slow to impose  border controls and do we need to go  further now  well i'm much happier now than i was  then because we have  put in place some important restrictions  and we've got quarantine hotels  on the way um and so on but the big  point here is that we have an  advantage because we're an island and  it's much easier for us to control  travel in and out of the country than it  is for countries with a lot of land  borders  and the lesson of this virus is that the  countries that were most successful were  the countries of east asia south korea  taiwan hong kong singapore  and they imposed travel restrictions  very very quickly  but that's not the only thing they did  they took the whole business  of quarantining people who might be  infectious  much more seriously and i would say that  the borders are one gap which i think we  are starting to plug  but another very very big one is the  fact that dido harding said  only this week 40 of the people who are  asked  to isolate by nhs test and trace don't  actually do so  and that works out at about 240 000  people  every week a number of whom are still  going to work incidentally so i think  one of the  big things we've got to look at is how  we are much much more effective at  getting people to isolate  who we need to how how do we do that  genuinely i mean i've just um  isolated for 10 days after uh  contracting covid and i was called by  test and trace very quickly they  called me fairly regularly throughout my  isolation but they only had my word that  i was staying at home  generally how do you actually make  people isolate well there are different  methods  around the world um in terms of the  enforcement of it um  regular contact i think is very  important and excellent that you had  that  there's also use of technology that they  they've been doing in poland and taiwan  but i think the single most important  thing uh is to  give people the confidence they're not  going to be out of pocket financially  and i think we do need to say to people  this is a public health matter if you  are out of pocket  in terms of your salary while you're in  that self-isolation period the  government will make up the difference i  think it just needs to be  very very straightforward so that people  understand they're not going to be out  of pocket  from following these instructions so the  current government scheme needs to go  further then  i think it does yes okay now on i want  to talk to you  very quickly about uh vaccinations and  teachers tony blair earlier on the show  also of course we know rachel ruiz from  labour calling for teachers to be moved  up  the vaccination cued to get schools open  more quickly tony blair thinks that you  could  potentially vaccinate all teachers in  just a couple of days  and do you think he's right well i think  it's a very compelling case i had a  discussion  on friday with vaccines minister nadim  zahawi with  some campaigners from goddamning who are  in my constituency very very keen to see  teachers vaccinated  um but you do need to look at the proper  scientific advice  and the chief executive of my local  hospital  the royal surrey tells me that her um  critical care beds are really full of  the people between  60 and 75 at the moment  and so for her in order to create more  capacity  it is a real priority to get that age  group vaccinated  so i think if we can do the teachers  everyone would want to do  that but we just need to listen to that  scientific advice  to make sure we get the balance  absolutely right now we are running out  of time but there was just one  sort of off-covered question that i was  keen to ask you  when i was researching the interview i  read uh the interview you did with the  observer last week  uh where you said that kia stammer  appeals to moderate people living in my  constituency in surrey  in a way that no labour leader since  blair has ever succeeded in doing  are you a bit worried about the threat  that kiersten poses to the conservative  party  well i think he's a very serious threat  a much bigger threat than  uh we've had for for many years indeed  since tony blair who you just had on  your show i would definitely say that  yes  but on the other hand this could be  a very exciting period for this country  and if you look at what is  i think generally recognized as the most  successful labour government since the  war the atlee government  they were running the country in a  period after a great  crisis the second world war but they  showed enormous imagination in the  setting up of the nhs in 1948  i think we could turn this into a 1948  moment for the country as we come out of  this crisis  by completely reforming the social care  system giving it a proper  10-year plan sorting out the workforce  issues in the nhs  getting our cancer survival rates to the  levels of france and germany  i think sometimes there are  opportunities in these terrible crises  and if we grasp those opportunities  boris johnson really can show the  country that we are the party of the nhs  which is something i know he  very much wants to do

